m=core.privacy.placeholderstore;

i=db:std.db;
i=str:std.str;
i=enc:std.encrypt;
i=encd:std.encoding;
i=log:shared.log;
i=s:core.storage.db;
i=ph:core.privacy.placeholder;

(* ══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
   X8.2 — the placeholder to value mapping, sealed at rest.

   WHAT THIS PROTECTS. The mapping is the table that turns an anonymised transcript
   back into named people. docs/threat-model.md:181 rates it Maximum, above the audit
   log and above the prompt cache, and until now it was plaintext in a SQLite file any
   process running as the user could read. Everything else loke does about privacy is
   worth less than this one table: a detector that misses an entity leaks one value,
   and a readable mapping table leaks every value loke ever detected, together with
   which request each belonged to.

   WHY FIELD LEVEL AND NOT DATABASE LEVEL. Database encryption is not available and
   that was settled under X8.1: the toolchain links plain `-lsqlite3`, plain SQLite
   accepts `PRAGMA key`, ignores it and reports success, and there is no SQLCipher
   anywhere in the toolchain. `core.storage.db.dbencryptionavailable()` returns false
   and says why. Nothing here reinstates a database-level claim.

   WHAT IS AND IS NOT PROTECTED, precisely, because a control described loosely is a
   control that gets over-claimed:

     Sealed  the original value, the placeholder, and the entity type — all three, as
             one payload. See "WHAT IS ENCRYPTED" below.
     Clear   the request id, the key version, and the created/expires timestamps.
     Clear   every other table in loke.db. audit_log, settings, sessions and the memory
             tables are untouched by this and remain plaintext on disk.

   So the honest sentence is "the placeholder mapping payload is encrypted at rest",
   not "loke's database is encrypted at rest". mappingencryptionstatus() below is the
   one place that sentence is written, so that anything asserting it derives it from
   here rather than restating it.

   ── WHAT IS ENCRYPTED, AND WHY NOT JUST THE VALUE ────────────────────────────
   The obvious design encrypts `original` and leaves `placeholder` and `entity_type`
   readable, because those are "just tokens". They are not.

     - The entity type IS an attribute of the subject. A row saying this request
       mentioned a MEDICAL_CONDITION, or a RELIGION, or a CRIMINAL_RECORD, discloses
       something about a person without disclosing their name — and under GDPR Art. 9
       the category is the sensitive part.
     - The placeholder EMBEDS the entity type and an ordinal: `[PERSON_a3f1_3]` says
       this request mentioned at least three people. Encrypting the type column while
       leaving the placeholder readable protects nothing, because the placeholder
       carries the type.
     - Together with a request id, a readable placeholder column gives a per-request
       census of entity types and counts. That is metadata about the subject of the
       request, derivable with no key at all.

   So all three fields go inside the AEAD, and the table below has no column any of
   them fits in. Restore does not need a searchable placeholder column: it asks for
   the mapping belonging to ONE request and decrypts it whole.

   ── ONE ROW PER REQUEST, AND THE LENGTH THAT IS LEFT ─────────────────────────
   One row per entity would make the row count for a request equal to the entity count
   for that request, so the census the previous paragraph closes would be back,
   readable by `SELECT COUNT() ... WHERE request_id=?`. One sealed payload per request
   removes it.

   Payload length still leaks, so the payload is padded with newlines to a 256-byte
   boundary before sealing. What that buys, stated exactly: maps that encode to under
   256 bytes — roughly the first half-dozen short entities — are indistinguishable in
   length from one another. A larger map still falls into a visible 256-byte bucket, so
   a 4KB payload does tell an observer that the request mentioned a lot of entities.
   That residual is not closed here and should not be described as closed.

   ── THE NONCE ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
   A fresh 12-byte nonce from `enc.aes256gcmnoncegen()` per SEAL, stored per row in
   nonce_hex. Reusing a nonce under one AES-GCM key is not untidy, it is catastrophic:
   two messages under the same key and nonce leak the XOR of their plaintexts and, via
   the shared GHASH subkey, allow forgery. The defence is structural rather than a rule
   somebody has to remember: `sealpayload` is the only function that encrypts, it
   generates the nonce itself, and it takes no nonce parameter. There is no way to call
   into this module with a nonce of your choosing. Re-storing a mapping for a request
   that already has one is an INSERT OR REPLACE that re-seals under a NEW nonce, which
   is the case a caller would otherwise get wrong.

   The nonce length is checked before use. `aes256gcmnoncegen` returns an empty array if
   its allocation fails, and a 0-byte nonce would be rejected by the AEAD but only after
   this code had decided it was encrypting something.

   ── KEY DERIVATION ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
   The keychain secret is never used as an AES key. It goes through HKDF-SHA256:

     ikm   the 64-character hex secret from core.storage.db.ensuredbkey, AS TEXT
     salt  "loke/placeholder-map/hkdf-salt/v1"   — a fixed, non-secret label
     info  "loke/placeholder-map/aes-256-gcm/v1" — purpose, algorithm, version
     len   32

   Three choices there, each with a reason:

   ikm as TEXT rather than the decoded 32 bytes. HKDF-Extract HMACs the ikm, so the 64
   hex characters carry exactly the same 256 bits of entropy as the 32 bytes they spell
   and the derived key is no weaker. What it avoids is a decode step on the KEY path:
   `encoding.hexdecode` is declared `[byte]!EncodingErr` but its wrapper returns a bare
   packed array with no error tag, so invalid hex arrives as `$ok` holding ZERO bytes.
   Decoding the secret would therefore turn a malformed keychain item into an empty
   ikm — a silently weak key — where the length check on the text rejects it outright.
   (That mismatch is measured, not inferred; see the note on hexbytes below.)

   A CONSTANT salt, not a stored random one. RFC 5869's salt randomises the extract
   step when the ikm is not uniformly random; here the ikm is `crypto.randombytes(32)`
   rendered as hex, so there is nothing left to randomise and a stored salt would add no
   entropy. It would add a failure mode: lose the salt row and the mapping table is
   permanently unreadable even with the keychain intact. The salt's remaining job is
   domain separation, which a constant does correctly and cannot lose.

   info carries purpose, algorithm and version. Any other subsystem deriving from the
   same keychain secret with a different info label gets an independent key, so a future
   compromise of one derived key does not compromise the others. Bumping the version
   rotates this key without touching the keychain, and key_version is stored per row so
   rows sealed under the old label remain decryptable while new rows use the new one.

   ── AUTHENTICATION, AND FAILING CLOSED ───────────────────────────────────────
   The AAD binds the ciphertext to its identity: domain label, key version, request id.
   A row lifted out of request A and pasted into request B's row therefore fails to
   authenticate rather than decrypting into the wrong person's mapping. Measured: wrong
   AAD returns err set and a zero-length plaintext.

   `enc.aes256gcmdecrypt` returns `DecryptResult`, a plain two-field record — NOT a sum
   type — so `result.err` is read directly and there is no T-18 exposure on the read
   path at all. A failure is distinguished from a success by `err` being non-empty AND,
   independently, by the plaintext being empty; both are checked, because a control that
   depends on one field of one struct being right is a control with one point of failure.

   FAILING CLOSED, for restoration, means returning the text UNCHANGED with its
   placeholders still in it. That is the safe direction and it is worth being explicit
   about why: the alternative failure — substituting whatever a partially decrypted or
   mis-keyed payload yielded — would put a real name where it does not belong, which is
   the PL1.1 defect (Bob Jones's action attributed to Alice Smith) reintroduced by the
   storage layer. A visible `[PERSON_a3f1_1]` in an answer is a user-facing annoyance; a
   confidently wrong name is a disclosure. Nothing in this module returns a partially
   restored string.

   NOTHING HERE LOGS A VALUE. The log lines carry a request id and a reason; no original,
   no placeholder, no entity type, no ciphertext.

   ── WHAT THIS DOES NOT DEFEND AGAINST ────────────────────────────────────────
   An attacker who can read the keychain as the user, or read loke's memory while it
   runs, or execute code as the user, gets the key and therefore the mapping. This
   control is against a copied database file, a backup, a synced folder, a stolen disk
   and every other process on the machine that can read the file but not the keychain.
   That is a real and common threat and it is also the whole of what is claimed.
   ══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════ *)

(* ══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
   PL1.3 — placeholder scoping, decided.

   THE DECISION: per-request salted scoping, with an opaque type-tagged surrogate.
   Confirming the 2026-09-20 plan's option N on the first axis and REVISING it on the
   second. Recorded here rather than in a document because this module is what makes
   scoping observable — persistence is the thing that could make a placeholder outlive
   the request that created it — and a decision recorded only in prose is a decision the
   next writer can contradict without noticing.

   The decision has to be made BEFORE persistence, not after, because the stored row's
   key is what fixes the scope. A row keyed by request id can only ever answer "what did
   THIS request mean by this placeholder", which is per-request scoping enforced by the
   schema. Choosing session or global scoping later would mean re-keying the table, i.e.
   a migration over the most sensitive data loke holds.

   FIRST AXIS — scope. Per request. The salt is a 4-hex-character prefix of the request
   id, landed under PL1.1 in core.privacy.placeholder.makesaltedplaceholder, so the same
   real value gets a different surrogate in every request.

   THE LINKAGE CONSEQUENCE, stated as the definitional trade-off it is and not as a
   research finding — `docs/research/disclosure-measurement-findings.md` §8 item 3 is
   explicit that "consistent placeholders create a linkage channel" is a definition, so
   this project must not present it as something it discovered:

     A placeholder that is CONSISTENT across requests IS a join key. That is what
     consistency means. Two prompts a month apart that both contain `[PERSON_7f21_1]`
     tell the recipient that they concern the same individual, without either prompt
     containing a name. The recipient — a provider, or anyone reading provider-side
     logs — can then accumulate attributes against that key across every request the
     user ever sends, and the accumulation is easier to work with than the names would
     have been, because the key is exact where names are ambiguous.

     A placeholder that is PER REQUEST costs exactly the mirror of that: cross-request
     utility. A follow-up question cannot refer back to the person in yesterday's
     prompt, because yesterday's surrogate is not today's. Multi-turn coherence within
     one request is unaffected — one request is one salt.

   Per request is chosen because the cost falls on convenience and the benefit falls on
   disclosure, and because a linkage channel is invisible to the user who is paying for
   it while a broken follow-up is not. Also note the defence is not novel: published work
   already scopes mappings per conversation (§2 of the same document). Do not describe it
   as an innovation.

   SECOND AXIS — surrogate style. An opaque type-tagged token, `[PERSON_a3f1_3]`, NOT a
   type-consistent realistic surrogate ("Michael Brown" for "Bob Jones"). This revises
   the plan's "type-consistent surrogates", and the reason is §8 items 1 and 2: the
   project may not claim that surrogates preserve utility better than redaction, and may
   not claim an LLM adversary cannot reverse them. Both of those are the only arguments
   for realistic surrogates, and loke has measured neither. A realistic surrogate also
   creates a failure mode an opaque one cannot: if restoration fails — and it CAN fail,
   see the fail-closed note above — an opaque placeholder is visibly unresolved, whereas
   a realistic surrogate reads as a real answer about a real person who does not exist.
   That is a fabrication delivered with a straight face, and it is worse than the
   annoyance it was meant to prevent.

   WHAT IS NOT CLAIMED. Per-request scoping does not make the transcript anonymous, does
   not prevent attribute inference from a single request, and does not prevent an
   adversary from reversing a surrogate using context the prompt itself supplies. It
   removes ONE channel: the cross-request join key. Nothing more.
   ══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════ *)

(* The decrypted mapping, plus a predicate — a plain struct, not a result type.

   T-18: `mt` on a user-declared error side ALWAYS takes the first arm, so `@$placeholderentry!$lokeerr`
   would report every decryption failure as a successful empty mapping, and an empty
   mapping restores nothing while looking exactly like a request that had no entities.
   That is the failure mode this whole module exists to avoid, so the failure is carried
   as a field every caller has to read. `openedwhy` never contains a value, only a
   reason. *)
t=$openedmap{
  openedentries:@$placeholderentry;
  openedok:bool;
  openedwhy:str
};

(* The stored form of one sealed payload. `sealedok` false means nothing may be written:
   see storemappingwithsecret, which refuses rather than writing a row whose payload it
   could not seal. A row with an empty payload_hex would look like a mapping that exists
   and restore to nothing. *)
t=$sealedpayload{
  sealednonce:str;
  sealedhex:str;
  sealedok:bool
};

f=hkdfsalt():str{
  <"loke/placeholder-map/hkdf-salt/v1"
};

f=hkdfinfo():str{
  <"loke/placeholder-map/aes-256-gcm/v1"
};

(* Stored per row so the info label above can change without a migration that rewrites
   ciphertext. Bumping it requires keeping the old label reachable for old rows. *)
f=keyversion():i64{
  <1
};

f=payloadversion():str{
  <"v1"
};

(* Padding block for the sealed payload, in bytes. See the length note in the header. *)
f=padblock():i64{
  <256
};

(* The additional authenticated data: domain, key version, request id. Authenticated but
   not encrypted, which is what binds a ciphertext to the row it was written for. *)
f=aadfor(requestid:str):str{
  let head=str.concat("loke/placeholder-map/v1|k";str.fromint(keyversion()));
  <str.concat(head;str.concat("|";requestid))
};

f=mappingencryptionstatus():str{
  <"placeholder mapping payloads are sealed at rest with AES-256-GCM, per-record nonce, key derived by HKDF-SHA256 from the OS keychain secret. The original value, the placeholder and the entity type are all inside the sealed payload; the request id, key version and timestamps are not. This covers ONE table: every other table in loke.db is plaintext on disk"
};

(* The AES key. 32 bytes, or an empty array when the secret is unusable — every caller
   checks the length before it encrypts or decrypts anything, because a short key means
   no key and no key must mean no operation. *)
f=derivemapkey(secrethex:str):@byte{
  if(str.len(secrethex)!=64){
    <@()
  }el{};
  <enc.hkdfsha256(str.bytes(secrethex);str.bytes(hkdfsalt());str.bytes(hkdfinfo());32)
};

f=keyusable(secrethex:str):bool{
  let key=derivemapkey(secrethex);
  <(key.len as i64)==32
};

f=hexof(value:str):str{
  <encd.hexencode(str.bytes(value))
};

(* Hex to bytes, with the length checked rather than trusted.

   `encoding.hexdecode` is declared `[byte]!EncodingErr`, which in this toolchain would
   normally discriminate — stdlib results pass 0 for the error side and `mt` reads that
   correctly. This one does not: the wrapper returns `tk_bytes_pack("",0)` for invalid
   input, which is a non-zero handle, so invalid hex arrives in the `$ok` arm holding
   zero bytes and the `$err` arm is unreachable. Measured, not read off the interface:
   `hexdecode("zz")` and `hexdecode("abc")` both return length 0 through `$ok`.

   So the check is arithmetic. n hex characters must decode to exactly n/2 bytes; an odd
   length, a non-hex character or a truncated column all fail that, and all of them
   return an empty array here, which the callers treat as no data. *)
f=hexbytes(hex:str):@byte{
  let want=(str.len(hex) as i64)/2;
  let out=mt encd.hexdecode(hex) {
    $ok:b b;
    $err:e @()
  };
  if((out.len as i64)!=want){
    <@()
  }el{};
  <out
};

f=hextext(hex:str):str{
  let raw=hexbytes(hex);
  if((raw.len as i64)==0){
    <""
  }el{};
  <mt str.frombytes(raw) {
    $ok:text text;
    $err:e ""
  }
};

(* ── the payload codec ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
   One line per mapping, three hex fields separated by `|`:

     v1
     <entitytypehex>|<placeholderhex>|<originalhex>
     ...
     (newline padding to the next 256-byte boundary)

   Every field is hex-encoded, which is what makes the framing unambiguous: hex is
   [0-9a-f] only, so neither `|` nor a newline can occur inside a field however peculiar
   a detected value is. A name containing a pipe, a newline or a NUL byte round-trips.
   The cost is that the payload is twice the size of the text it holds, which for a
   mapping list is nothing.

   Padding is newlines, so it needs no length header and no trailing marker: a blank line
   is not a record and the parser skips it. `str.split` on "\n" yields the empty strings
   at the end, verified.

   PARSE FAILURES ARE CODEC DEFECTS, NOT TAMPERING. This payload is only ever parsed
   AFTER the GCM tag has verified, and a verified tag means the bytes are exactly the
   bytes that were sealed. So a malformed line cannot have been produced by an attacker
   without the key; it can only have been produced by a bug in this codec. decodepayload
   therefore fails the whole mapping rather than skipping the bad line, so such a bug
   surfaces as a refusal instead of as a silently shorter mapping. *)
f=encodeentry(entry:$placeholderentry):str{
  let typehex=hexof(entry.entitytype);
  let phhex=hexof(entry.placeholder);
  let valhex=hexof(entry.original);
  <str.concat(typehex;str.concat("|";str.concat(phhex;str.concat("|";valhex))))
};

f=encodepayload(map:$placeholdermap):str{
  let lines=mut.@();
  lines=lines.push(payloadversion());
  lp(let i=0;i<map.entries.len;i=i+1){
    let entry:$placeholderentry=map.entries.get(i);
    lines=lines.push(encodeentry(entry))
  };
  (* T-23: separator first. *)
  <str.concat(str.join("\n";lines);"\n")
};

f=paddedpayload(payload:str):str{
  let block=padblock();
  let len=str.len(payload) as i64;
  let whole=(len/block)*block;
  let rem=len-whole;
  if(rem==0){
    <payload
  }el{
    <str.concat(payload;str.repeat("\n";(block-rem) as u64))
  }
};

f=failedmap(why:str):$openedmap{
  <$openedmap{openedentries:@();openedok:false;openedwhy:why}
};

f=decodepayload(payload:str):$openedmap{
  let lines=str.split(payload;"\n");
  if((lines.len as i64)==0){
    <failedmap("the sealed payload was empty")
  }el{};
  if(str.eq(lines.get(0);payloadversion())){}el{
    <failedmap("the sealed payload carries an unrecognised format version")
  };
  let out=mut.@();
  let malformed=mut.0;
  let i=mut.1;
  lp(let lv=0;true;lv=lv){
    if(i>=(lines.len as i64)){
      br
    }el{
      let line=lines.get(i);
      if(str.len(line)==0){}el{
        let parts=str.split(line;"|");
        if((parts.len as i64)==3){
          out=out.push($placeholderentry{
            original:hextext(parts.get(2));
            placeholder:hextext(parts.get(1));
            entitytype:hextext(parts.get(0))
          })
        }el{
          malformed=malformed+1
        }
      };
      i=i+1
    }
  };
  if(malformed>0){
    <failedmap("a record line in an authenticated payload did not parse, which is a codec defect rather than tampering")
  }el{};
  <$openedmap{openedentries:out;openedok:true;openedwhy:""}
};

(* ── sealing and opening ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────
   sealpayload is the only function in loke that encrypts a mapping, and it takes no
   nonce: the nonce is generated here, once per call, so no call site can supply or reuse
   one. *)
f=nosealedpayload():$sealedpayload{
  <$sealedpayload{sealednonce:"";sealedhex:"";sealedok:false}
};

f=sealpayload(secrethex:str;requestid:str;payload:str):$sealedpayload{
  let key=derivemapkey(secrethex);
  if((key.len as i64)!=32){
    <nosealedpayload()
  }el{};
  let nonce=enc.aes256gcmnoncegen();
  if((nonce.len as i64)!=12){
    <nosealedpayload()
  }el{};
  let sealed=enc.aes256gcmencrypt(key;nonce;str.bytes(payload);str.bytes(aadfor(requestid)));
  (* The tag is 16 bytes and the payload is never empty, so anything at or below 16 bytes
     is the AEAD's own failure path returning an empty array, not a short message. *)
  if((sealed.len as i64)<=16){
    <nosealedpayload()
  }el{};
  <$sealedpayload{sealednonce:encd.hexencode(nonce);sealedhex:encd.hexencode(sealed);sealedok:true}
};

f=openpayload(secrethex:str;requestid:str;noncehex:str;payloadhex:str):$openedmap{
  let key=derivemapkey(secrethex);
  if((key.len as i64)!=32){
    <failedmap("no usable mapping key is available")
  }el{};
  let nonce=hexbytes(noncehex);
  if((nonce.len as i64)!=12){
    <failedmap("the stored nonce is not twelve bytes")
  }el{};
  let sealed=hexbytes(payloadhex);
  if((sealed.len as i64)<=16){
    <failedmap("the stored payload is too short to carry an authentication tag")
  }el{};
  let result=enc.aes256gcmdecrypt(key;nonce;sealed;str.bytes(aadfor(requestid)));
  (* Two independent checks, deliberately. A wrong key, a tampered payload, a payload
     moved to another request's row and a truncated column all land here. *)
  if(str.len(result.err)==0){}el{
    <failedmap("authentication failed: the payload was sealed under a different key, for a different request, or has been altered")
  };
  if((result.ok.len as i64)==0){
    <failedmap("decryption produced no plaintext")
  }el{};
  let payload=mt str.frombytes(result.ok) {
    $ok:text text;
    $err:e ""
  };
  if(str.len(payload)==0){
    <failedmap("the decrypted payload could not be read")
  }el{};
  <decodepayload(payload)
};

(* ── persistence ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
   INSERT OR REPLACE, so re-storing a mapping for a request re-seals it under a NEW
   nonce rather than leaving the old row alongside. That is the nonce-reuse case a
   caller would otherwise have to remember. *)
f=upsertsql():str{
  <"INSERT OR REPLACE INTO placeholder_map_records (request_id,key_version,nonce_hex,payload_hex,created_at,expires_at) VALUES (?,?,?,?,datetime('now'),datetime('now','+1 day'))"
};

(* Takes the secret as a parameter. X8.3: production resolves it from the keychain in
   storemapping below, and tests pass one of their own, so no ambient state anywhere can
   redirect which key seals a row. A secret that is not 64 characters is refused by
   derivemapkey, so a trivial or empty one cannot be used by accident.

   Returns a plain bool. T-18 would make the error arm of a `bool!$lokeerr` dead, and
   "the mapping was not stored" reading as success is how a restore path ends up quietly
   finding nothing. *)
f=storemappingwithsecret(requestid:str;map:$placeholdermap;secrethex:str):bool{
  if(str.len(requestid)==0){
    log.warn("placeholder store: refusing to store a mapping with no request id";"placeholder_store";"system");
    <false
  }el{};
  let sealed=sealpayload(secrethex;requestid;paddedpayload(encodepayload(map)));
  if(sealed.sealedok){}el{
    (* No row is written. A row whose payload could not be sealed would look like a
       mapping that exists and restore to nothing, which is worse than no row: the
       caller can see the false return, but a later reader cannot see the difference. *)
    log.warn("placeholder store: could not seal the mapping, so nothing was persisted";"placeholder_store";requestid);
    <false
  };
  let wrote=mt db.exec(upsertsql();@(requestid;str.fromint(keyversion());sealed.sealednonce;sealed.sealedhex)) {
    $ok:v true;
    $err:e false
  };
  if(wrote){
    log.debug("placeholder store: sealed mapping persisted";"placeholder_store";requestid)
  }el{
    log.warn("placeholder store: failed to persist a sealed mapping";"placeholder_store";requestid)
  };
  <wrote
};

(* The production entry point. The key comes from the keychain, unconditionally, with no
   branch on anything: core.storage.db.ensuredbkey returns "" when the keychain cannot
   supply one, derivemapkey refuses "", sealpayload refuses a short key and this refuses
   to write. A missing key fails the operation; it never falls back to plaintext. *)
f=storemapping(requestid:str;map:$placeholdermap):bool{
  <storemappingwithsecret(requestid;map;s.ensuredbkey())
};

f=readmappingwithsecret(requestid:str;secrethex:str):$openedmap{
  (* db.one returns the error side when there is no row, which is the common case for a
     request that had no entities — not a failure worth a warning. *)
  <mt db.one("SELECT nonce_hex,payload_hex FROM placeholder_map_records WHERE request_id=?";@(requestid)) {
    $ok:r openpayload(secrethex;requestid;s.rowstr(r;"nonce_hex");s.rowstr(r;"payload_hex"));
    $err:e failedmap("no sealed mapping is stored for this request")
  }
};

f=readmapping(requestid:str):$openedmap{
  <readmappingwithsecret(requestid;s.ensuredbkey())
};

(* Restoration, failing closed by returning the text UNCHANGED. See the header: a
   placeholder left visible is an annoyance, a wrong name substituted confidently is a
   disclosure, and there is no middle state in which a partially restored string is
   returned.

   The substitution itself delegates to core.privacy.placeholder.restore rather than
   looping here. A second copy of that loop is precisely how PL1.1's collision stayed
   invisible for as long as it did — two substitution paths, one of them wrong. *)
f=restorewithsecret(requestid:str;text:str;secrethex:str):str{
  let opened=readmappingwithsecret(requestid;secrethex);
  if(opened.openedok){}el{
    log.warn(str.concat("placeholder store: text left unrestored: ";opened.openedwhy);"placeholder_store";requestid);
    <text
  };
  <ph.restore(text;$placeholdermap{entries:opened.openedentries})
};

f=restoretext(requestid:str;text:str):str{
  <restorewithsecret(requestid;text;s.ensuredbkey())
};

(* ── deletion (X8.4) ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
   Dropping the row is only half of it, and the half that is easy to believe. The proof
   that the bytes are gone is a filesystem assertion, not an interface promise, and it
   lives in tests/unit/privacy/test_mapping_deletion.tk: after deleting a subject's
   mapping, no file under the data directory — the database, its rollback journal, any
   WAL or -shm — contains the plaintext of the deleted value.

   Two things make that assertion pass, and they are worth separating because only one of
   them is about deletion:

     1. The plaintext was never written. The payload is sealed before it reaches SQLite,
        so the value is not in the file even BEFORE deletion, and not in a freed page, a
        journal, a WAL frame, a temp file or a backup of any of them. This is the load
        bearing half.
     2. PRAGMA secure_delete, set on the connection by core.storage.db.openstore, zeroes
        the freed pages so the CIPHERTEXT goes too. Measured to be honoured by this
        toolchain rather than assumed — the measurement is recorded in that module,
        because PRAGMA key is exactly why nothing here trusts a pragma it has not tested.

   Deleting the keychain secret would also make every remaining row unreadable, and that
   is a genuine third layer, but it is not deletion: it destroys access to the data rather
   than the data. It is not claimed here as deletion.

   std.db.exec cannot report zero rows affected — its wrapper returns 1 for any success
   and 0 only for an error — so `true` here means the statement ran, not that a row
   existed. Callers wanting the distinction check countforrequest first. *)
f=deletemapping(requestid:str):bool{
  let deleted=mt db.exec("DELETE FROM placeholder_map_records WHERE request_id=?";@(requestid)) {
    $ok:v true;
    $err:e false
  };
  if(deleted){
    log.info("placeholder store: mapping deleted";"placeholder_store";requestid)
  }el{
    log.warn("placeholder store: failed to delete a mapping";"placeholder_store";requestid)
  };
  <deleted
};

f=purgeallmappings():bool{
  let purged=mt db.exec("DELETE FROM placeholder_map_records";@()) {
    $ok:v true;
    $err:e false
  };
  if(purged){
    log.info("placeholder store: every stored mapping was purged";"placeholder_store";"maintenance")
  }el{
    log.warn("placeholder store: failed to purge stored mappings";"placeholder_store";"maintenance")
  };
  <purged
};

(* The previous body was `db.exec(store.conn; sql)` with `store:i64` — a field access on
   an integer AND the wrong arity for std.db, which takes (sql, params) and uses the
   connection db.open established. It type-checked: the connection pointer was read as
   the SQL string and the SQL string as the parameter array. It crashed the first time it
   ran, and nothing ran it, because no caller in the repository called this module.

   `expires_at < datetime('now')` is a string comparison and it is safe here, which is
   NOT true of audit_log.created_at (GA5.11). Both sides of this one are produced by
   SQLite's own datetime(), whose format is zero-padded `YYYY-MM-DD HH:MM:SS`, so
   lexicographic order is chronological order. created_at there is epoch milliseconds
   compared against ISO dates, where "1790..." sorts before "2026-...", which is why that
   one needed a CAST. Nothing digests this column, so there is no hash to disturb. *)
f=expireold():bool{
  let expired=mt db.exec("DELETE FROM placeholder_map_records WHERE expires_at IS NOT NULL AND expires_at < datetime('now')";@()) {
    $ok:v true;
    $err:e false
  };
  if(expired){
    log.info("placeholder store: expired mappings removed";"placeholder_store";"maintenance")
  }el{
    log.warn("placeholder store: failed to expire old mappings";"placeholder_store";"maintenance")
  };
  <expired
};

(* ── counts ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
   Two counts, and the difference between them is the point of the schema.

   countforrequest counts sealed RECORDS, so it is 0 or 1: it answers "is there a mapping
   for this request" and nothing more. It previously declared `u32!$lokeerr`, whose error
   arm T-18 makes dead — every caller would have read the first arm whatever happened —
   and it counted per-entity rows, which is the census this design removes.

   entrycountforrequest counts MAPPINGS, and needs the key. That asymmetry is deliberate:
   how many entities a request mentioned is not readable from the database file. *)
f=countforrequest(requestid:str):i64{
  <s.countquery("SELECT request_id FROM placeholder_map_records WHERE request_id=?";@(requestid))
};

f=entrycountforrequestwithsecret(requestid:str;secrethex:str):i64{
  let opened=readmappingwithsecret(requestid;secrethex);
  if(opened.openedok){}el{
    <0-1
  };
  <opened.openedentries.len as i64
};

f=entrycountforrequest(requestid:str):i64{
  <entrycountforrequestwithsecret(requestid;s.ensuredbkey())
};
